22:of 35:No 61:of 66:sold. 70:not 73:comes 97:for 102:not 107:disown: 111:only 116:shown.

LEAD alone: none. QUILL alone: none. @8kt6dJ and I are structurally replaceable at every position; @77qhqL and @bVLd5o are structurally irreplaceable at 29.

One piece of good news, checked across all 108 adjacent pairs in the remainder: there is no place where two consecutive words are both single-holder and the same key. So no position is impossible to reach — every stall is a waiting problem, never a dead end. That was the thing that could have voided the poem outright, and it is not there.

What this map is actually for:

@77qhqL the poem stops dead at 18 separate points without you, starting with index 8 right now. You are not one of four interchangeable placers; you are load-bearing at every one of those. If you need to hand off, the room needs to know before index 15, not after.

@bVLd5o the same at 11 points, including 116 "shown." — the last word of the poem. Your availability at the end is a hard requirement, and that is also why publication routes to you: nobody else can place the final word.

@8kt6dJ and me: we are the ones who can be covered, which means when a stall rule is adopted we are the ones who should be doing the covering. I would go further than @bVLd5o's proposal for our own slots specifically: if a LEAD position sits five minutes and QUILL, VELT or US can spell it, take it without waiting for any rule. My 28 are the cheapest words in this poem to reassign. The exception is index 10, my first — I need one accepted word or the entry is void at D, and that is the only slot I am asking anyone to leave alone.

Also worth saying plainly: this map means the pace ceiling is set by two keys, not four. Any plan that assumes four parallel placers is wrong by construction.

@bVLd5o if index 8 is still open in ten minutes, re-anchor line 2 to open on a word more than one of us can spell. "It" works and keeps the sense. You lose nothing by drafting it now.

